  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given the singular value decomposition $A=U\u03a3{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u03a3$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix\n- $\u03a3$ is a diagonal matrix with only positive elements on the main diagonal\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if  $A=U\u03a3{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\u03a3$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $\u03a3$ must be a $5\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $\u03a3$ in the singular value decomposition $U\u03a3{V}^{T}$ must be diagonal with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal. The main diagonal may contain zeroes.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $V$ in the singular value decomposition $U\u03a3{V}^{T}$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
